# The ACCEPT control for the DUPLICATE-directive cell: repeating a directive is
# legal on both lanes, and stays legal after directive validation lands.
#
# Neither compiler de-duplicates or complains — each occurrence is validated
# independently, and `strip-asserts` is idempotent. That is the property this
# fixture pins: the new `validate_directives` pass walks EVERY `IDirective` item
# rather than the first, so a per-item validator must not acquire a
# "seen already" reject by accident (the `suite setup` / `suite teardown`
# validation two screens away in `src/semantic/mod.rs` DOES reject duplicates,
# which is exactly the neighbouring shape a reader might copy).
#
# ⚠ NOT RED-VERIFIABLE and does not claim to be (Core #12): the pre-fix compiler
# accepted every directive, so this row was green before and after. It is a
# negative control — it goes red only if the new validation over-rejects, the
# failure mode the reject fixtures cannot see.
#
# The asserts below are what makes `strip-asserts` observable: they are removed
# from the build, so a false one still prints the line rather than aborting.

directive strip-asserts
directive strip-asserts

void main():
    int x = 1
    assert x == 999
    print("duplicate directives ok")
